      <div class="takeaways">
        <article><h3>1. Dominance is concentrated but not permanent.</h3><p>Only {len(repeat_composers)} composers repeat, and the top score is just {max_wins}; the Academy repeatedly forms short canons, then disperses recognition.</p></article>
        <article><h3>2. The 1950s are the densest full-decade repeat era.</h3><p>Half of the decade’s ceremonies include a prior winner, making it the clearest historical cluster of recurring mid-century names.</p></article>
        <article><h3>3. The 1980s and 1940s are the broadest winner pools.</h3><p>Both decades reach 12 unique individual composers, though through different mechanisms: early shared credits in the 1940s and stylistic/industrial expansion in the 1980s.</p></article>
        <article><h3>4. The 2020s are unusually concentrated so far.</h3><p>With a 71.4% repeat-winner ceremony rate across seven rows, the current decade suggests either a temporary cluster or a new prestige loop around known contemporary composers.</p></article>
        <article><h3>5. Shared-credit wins remain exceptional.</h3><p>Only {collab_ceremonies} of {total_ceremonies} rows list multiple credited composers, so the Oscar score story remains primarily organized around individual authorial identity.</p></article>
        <article><h3>6. Style must be handled cautiously.</h3><p>The CSV lacks genre or musical-style fields; film-title and era interpretation can contextualize trends, but cannot support quantified style claims.</p></article>
